1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,403
What actually moves your credit score?

2
00:00:02,823 --> 00:00:07,335
Not your income, and not checking your own score. Here's what really counts, in numbers.

3
00:00:08,105 --> 00:00:13,197
The common FICO score runs from 300 to 850, and it's built from five parts.

4
00:00:13,617 --> 00:00:14,703
Here's the recipe.

5
00:00:15,123 --> 00:00:20,809
Payment history, thirty-five percent. Amounts owed, thirty percent. Length of history, fifteen.

6
00:00:21,229 --> 00:00:24,853
New credit and credit mix make up the last 20%: 10% each.

7
00:00:25,623 --> 00:00:26,792
Two of these matter most.

8
00:00:27,212 --> 00:00:39,181
Payment history is the biggest piece. A payment 30 days late can stay on your report for up to seven years. The simplest fix: autopay at least the minimum on every card, then pay more by hand.

9
00:00:39,951 --> 00:00:42,845
Second, amounts owed, mostly your utilization.

10
00:00:43,265 --> 00:00:52,498
Utilization is your balance divided by your limit. Our episode one card has $3,200 on a $5,000 limit: 64%.

11
00:00:52,918 --> 00:00:58,174
Lower is better. Under 30% is a common guideline, and under 10% is even better.

12
00:00:58,594 --> 00:01:01,879
Here's the paycheck plan from episode one, month by month.

13
00:01:02,299 --> 00:01:09,037
By month five, it's under thirty percent. By month eight, zero. Paying down debt helps your score as a side effect.

14
00:01:09,807 --> 00:01:10,964
The other three, quickly.

15
00:01:11,384 --> 00:01:23,885
Length of history: keep your oldest card open, even if you barely use it. New credit: each application can mean a hard inquiry, so space them out. Credit mix matters least. Never borrow just to improve it.

16
00:01:24,305 --> 00:01:30,741
And checking your own score or report doesn't hurt it. You can get free reports at AnnualCreditReport.com.

17
00:01:31,511 --> 00:01:41,578
The Paycheck Planner in the description shows your payoff timeline, which is also your utilization timeline. This is general education, not financial advice.

18
00:01:41,998 --> 00:01:44,753
Subscribe for more money math, with real numbers.
